===Etymology===
{{dercat|ang|gem-pro|ine-pro|inh=2}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*þornu|t=thorn, sloe}}.

{{rel-top|Cognates}}
Germanic cognates include {{cog|osx|thorn}} ({{cog|nds|Dorn}}, {{m|nds|Doorn}}), {{cog|nl|doorn}}, {{cog|goh|thorn}} ({{cog|de|Dorn}}), {{cog|non|þorn}} ({{cog|sv|törne}}), {{cog|got|𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌿𐍃}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{cog|cu|трънъ|sc=Cyrs}} ({{cog|ru|тёрн||sloe, blackthorn}}, {{cog|sk|tŕň}}), {{cog|sa|तृण||grass}}. {{rel-bottom}}

